      "writtenOn": "2018-08-21T17:39:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "mnissler@chromium.org This is how I am hiding the symbols. It doesn\u0027t make them weak, it converts functions with __attribute((visibility(\"hidden\"))) to static functions that are linked internally. This is different than weak symbols which can be overridden at link time.\n\nIf you use objdump to look at the resulting object file, it no longer shows the symbols that were localized whereas weak symbols would be present unless I am misunderstanding something.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 56,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 56,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "e020178ab623913aa37571fc99269b1132b3ad27",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "77da5065_584c9f7f",
        "filename": "Makefile.rules",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 56,
      "author": {
        "id": 1160200
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-22T18:04:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To continue our previous discussion. It is possible to hide/locally link these symbols, and export a white-listed set of symbols, but this would be messy in cases where the fuzzer uses internal structs and functions outside of the primary entry point.\n\nThe particular way I implemented this makes it fairly easy to write unit tests or fuzzing targets that use external libraries, but interact with the EC code at a low level without having to maintain a white-list or duplicate set of headers.",
      "parentUuid": "f6c06602_68fe3d65",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 56,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 56,
        "endChar": 29
      },
